## PUT vs PATCH

## The Core Difference

```
PUT   → Replace the whole resource
PATCH → Modify part of the resource
```

## Example

```typescript
// Current user record:
// { id: 1, name: 'Aaron', email: 'a@b.com', role: 'admin' }

// PUT /users/1 — replace entirely
fetch('/users/1', {
  method: 'PUT',
  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Aaron Kisac' }), // email and role not included
});
// Result: { id: 1, name: 'Aaron Kisac', email: null, role: null } ❌

// PATCH /users/1 — partial update
fetch('/users/1', {
  method: 'PATCH',
  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Aaron Kisac' }),
});
// Result: { id: 1, name: 'Aaron Kisac', email: 'a@b.com', role: 'admin' } ✅
```

## Idempotency

```
Idempotent = calling N times has same effect as calling once

PUT  → Always idempotent: PUT /users/1 { name: 'Aaron' } sets name to Aaron
        regardless of how many times you call it.

PATCH → Depends on implementation
        PATCH { increment: true } is NOT idempotent
        PATCH { name: 'Aaron' } IS idempotent
```

## When To Use What

```
PUT  → File uploads (replace entire file)
       Config updates where you always send the full shape
       When idempotency guarantee matters (retry logic)

PATCH → Form field updates (change email only)
        Sparse updates on large objects
        Status transitions (set status: 'archived')
```

## CORS — Cross-Origin Resource Sharing

## What Triggers CORS

```
Same Origin: protocol + host + port all match
  Frontend: https://app.com
  API:      https://app.com/api  ← same origin, no CORS

Cross Origin: any part differs
  Frontend: https://app.com
  API:      https://api.com      ← CORS applies
  API:      http://app.com       ← different protocol
  API:      https://app.com:3001 ← different port
```

## Preflight Request Flow

```
1. Browser sees fetch('https://api.com/data', { method: 'POST' })
2. Browser sends preflight:
   OPTIONS /data HTTP/1.1
   Origin: https://app.com
   Access-Control-Request-Method: POST

3. Server responds:
   Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.com
   Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, DELETE
   Access-Control-Max-Age: 86400

4. Browser sends the real POST request
```

## Fixing CORS in Next.js

```typescript
// next.config.ts
export default {
  async headers() {
    return [
      {
        source: '/api/:path*',
        headers: [
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin', value: 'https://app.com' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Methods', value: 'GET,POST,DELETE' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers', value: 'Content-Type,Authorization' },
        ],
      },
    ];
  },
};

// Or in a Route Handler
export async function OPTIONS() {
  return new Response(null, {
    headers: {
      'Access-Control-Allow-Origin': '*',
      'Access-Control-Allow-Methods': 'GET, POST',
    },
  });
}
```

## Security Note

```
Never use Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * for credentialed requests
(cookies, Authorization header). Use a specific origin allowlist.
```

## Authentication vs Authorisation

## The Core Distinction

```
Authentication (AuthN) = Identity
  'Who are you?'
  Verifying credentials (password, biometric, token)
  Happens first

Authorisation (AuthZ) = Permission
  'What can you do?'
  Checking if the verified identity has access
  Happens after authentication
```

## HTTP Status Codes

```
401 Unauthorized → Not authenticated
  (Misleadingly named — should be 'Unauthenticated')
  Include WWW-Authenticate header to tell client how to auth

403 Forbidden → Authenticated but not authorised
  I know who you are; you're not allowed to do this
```

## RBAC Example

```typescript
type Role = 'admin' | 'editor' | 'viewer';

const permissions: Record<Role, string[]> = {
  admin: ['read', 'write', 'delete', 'manage-users'],
  editor: ['read', 'write'],
  viewer: ['read'],
};

function can(user: { role: Role }, action: string): boolean {
  return permissions[user.role].includes(action);
}

// Usage in a route handler
if (!can(user, 'delete')) {
  return Response.json({ error: 'Forbidden' }, { status: 403 });
}
```

## ABAC Example (Attribute-Based)

```typescript
// More flexible: decisions based on user + resource + context
function canEditPost(user: User, post: Post, now: Date): boolean {
  return (
    user.id === post.authorId ||    // own post
    user.role === 'admin' ||         // or admin
    now < post.lockDate              // and within edit window
  );
}
```

## API Pagination

## 1. Offset Pagination

```
GET /posts?page=3&limit=20

DB: SELECT * FROM posts ORDER BY id LIMIT 20 OFFSET 60

✅ Simple to implement
✅ Can jump to any page
❌ Slow on large datasets (full scan to offset)
❌ Inconsistent: if item inserted during pagination, items shift

Response:
{
  "data": [...],
  "pagination": { "page": 3, "limit": 20, "total": 1240, "pages": 62 }
}
```

## 2. Cursor Pagination

```
GET /posts?cursor=eyJpZCI6MTAwfQ&limit=20
(cursor = base64 of { id: 100 })

DB: SELECT * FROM posts WHERE id > 100 ORDER BY id LIMIT 20

✅ O(log n) — uses index seek, stays fast at any depth
✅ Stable — inserts don't shift results
❌ Can't jump to page N
❌ More complex to implement

Response:
{
  "data": [...],
  "pagination": {
    "nextCursor": "eyJpZCI6MTIwfQ",
    "hasMore": true
  }
}
```

## 3. Implementation in Next.js

```typescript
// Cursor-based with Supabase
export async function GET(req: Request) {
  const { searchParams } = new URL(req.url);
  const cursor = searchParams.get('cursor');
  const limit = Math.min(Number(searchParams.get('limit') ?? 20), 100);

  let query = supabase
    .from('posts')
    .select('*')
    .order('id', { ascending: true })
    .limit(limit + 1); // fetch one extra to determine hasMore

  if (cursor) {
    const { id } = JSON.parse(atob(cursor));
    query = query.gt('id', id);
  }

  const { data } = await query;
  const hasMore = data!.length > limit;
  const items = data!.slice(0, limit);
  const nextCursor = hasMore ? btoa(JSON.stringify({ id: items.at(-1)!.id })) : null;

  return Response.json({ data: items, pagination: { nextCursor, hasMore } });
}
```
